1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,912
This is STAT 331, video 18. It is a review of the Midterm 1 question types.

2
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:13,826
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:14,276 --> 00:00:27,972
Type A: two groups, coded x equals 0 and x equals 1. You interpret beta 1, write the null hypothesis, derive the estimates, and test. This is Tutorial 2, question 2.

4
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:38,235
Type B: a new scale for x. You interpret the new slope and compare the p-values. This is Assignment 1, question 2, parts b and c.

5
00:00:38,685 --> 00:00:45,320
Type C: link a test and a confidence interval. This is Assignment 1, question 2, part a.

6
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:57,781
Section 1. Every Midterm 1 question starts from the simple linear regression model. y i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 x i, plus epsilon i.

7
00:00:58,231 --> 00:01:15,468
Theory questions ask you to derive a mean, a variance, or a distribution. Or they ask which model assumption a result uses. Tutorial 1, question 3, and Assignment 1, question 1, are of this type. Earlier videos solve them.

8
00:01:15,918 --> 00:01:28,953
Applied questions give a coefficient table or data summaries. You calculate t values, p-values, confidence intervals, and prediction intervals. Earlier videos also solve these.

9
00:01:29,403 --> 00:01:40,027
This video does three more types. Type A codes x as 0 or 1. Type B standardizes x. Type C links a test and a confidence interval.

10
00:01:40,477 --> 00:02:04,583
All three types use four formulas from Lectures 2 and 3. First, the slope estimate. beta 1 hat equals S x y over S x x. S x x is the sum of x i minus x bar, squared. S x y is the sum of x i minus x bar, times y i minus y bar. The green triangle shows the slope.

11
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:19,455
Second, the intercept estimate. beta 0 hat equals y bar, minus beta 1 hat times x bar. So the fitted line goes through the point x bar, y bar. The ring marks this point.

12
00:02:19,905 --> 00:02:36,374
Third, the standard error of beta 1 hat. It is sigma hat, over the square root of S x x. sigma hat squared is the sum of the squared residuals e i, over n minus 2. The orange segments are the residuals.

13
00:02:36,824 --> 00:02:50,413
Fourth, the t statistic. It is beta 1 hat minus theta 0, over the standard error. Under the null hypothesis, it has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

14
00:02:50,863 --> 00:03:05,455
Section 2. Question type A gives two groups. x is 0 in group 0, and 1 in group 1. Tutorial 2, question 2, is of this type. The picture shows an example with 4 and 3 values.

15
00:03:05,905 --> 00:03:20,028
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: put x equal to 0 and x equal to 1 into the mean of the model. This gives the two group means, beta 0, and beta 0 plus beta 1.

16
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:31,401
Step 2: beta 1 is the mean of group 1 minus the mean of group 0. No difference in means is the null hypothesis beta 1 equals 0.

17
00:03:31,851 --> 00:03:41,195
Step 3: use the 0 and 1 values to find x bar, S x x, and S x y. x bar is n 2 over n.

18
00:03:41,645 --> 00:03:51,138
Step 4: put them into the formulas for beta 1 hat and beta 0 hat. Simplify. The fitted line goes through both group means.

19
00:03:51,588 --> 00:04:04,089
Step 5: the t test. Find sigma hat squared from the residuals, then the standard error and t. Compare t with the t quantile with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

20
00:04:04,539 --> 00:04:13,179
Tutorial 2, question 2. The usual two-sample problem can be written as a regression problem. Pause the video and try it.

21
00:04:13,629 --> 00:04:28,541
The first n 1 subjects have x equal to 0. The other n 2 subjects have x equal to 1. So x only says which group a subject is in. In the picture, all points of a group are on one vertical line.

22
00:04:28,991 --> 00:04:37,823
y bar 0 is the sample mean of group 0. y bar 1 is the sample mean of group 1. The purple marks show them.

23
00:04:38,273 --> 00:04:54,956
The pictures use our own small example. Group 0 has the values 3, 5, 4, and 6. They add to 18, so the mean is 4.5. Group 1 has 8, 7, and 9. They add to 24, so the mean is 8.

24
00:04:55,406 --> 00:05:10,553
Part a. Step 1 of card A. Put x equal to 0 into the mean of the model. The mean response of group 0 is beta 0. Each group has a normal distribution around its mean, with variance sigma squared.

25
00:05:11,003 --> 00:05:17,125
Put x equal to 1. The mean response of group 1 is beta 0 plus beta 1.

26
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:33,362
Step 2. Subtract line 1 from line 2. beta 1 is the difference in mean response between group 1 and group 0. This is the slope interpretation of Lecture 2, with a step of one unit from 0 to 1.

27
00:05:33,812 --> 00:05:42,388
So the two groups have the same mean exactly when beta 1 is 0. The red curve shows group 1 under this hypothesis.

28
00:05:42,838 --> 00:05:53,611
The answer: beta 1 is the difference in means. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals 0. The alternative is beta 1 is not 0.

29
00:05:54,061 --> 00:06:11,235
Part b. Pause the video and try it. Step 3 of card A: find x bar and S x x from the 0 and 1 values. Line 1: x bar is the sum of the x i, over n. The picture shows n 1 zeros and n 2 ones.

30
00:06:11,685 --> 00:06:18,959
The zeros add nothing. Each one adds 1. So the sum is n 2, and x bar is n 2 over n.

31
00:06:19,409 --> 00:06:27,153
Now the deviations from x bar. For group 0, x i minus x bar is minus n 2 over n.

32
00:06:27,603 --> 00:06:35,881
For group 1, it is 1 minus n 2 over n. This is n 1 over n, because n minus n 2 is n 1.

33
00:06:36,331 --> 00:06:45,568
S x x adds the squared deviations. There are n 1 squares of n 2 over n, and n 2 squares of n 1 over n.

34
00:06:46,018 --> 00:06:52,269
Take out n 1 times n 2, over n squared. What is left is n 2 plus n 1.

35
00:06:52,719 --> 00:06:58,628
n 2 plus n 1 is n. So S x x is n 1 times n 2, over n.

36
00:06:59,078 --> 00:07:22,075
Check with the example. n 1 is 4 and n 2 is 3. x bar is 3 over 7. S x x is 4 times 9 over 49, plus 3 times 16 over 49. That is 84 over 49, which is 12 over 7. The formula gives 4 times 3 over 7, also 12 over 7.

37
00:07:22,525 --> 00:07:31,507
Still step 3: S x y. Line 1 is the definition. The picture shows the y values of the example in their groups.

38
00:07:31,957 --> 00:07:36,927
Multiply out the bracket y i minus y bar. We get two sums.

39
00:07:37,377 --> 00:07:50,604
The deviations x i minus x bar add to 0. So the second sum is y bar times 0. S x y is the sum of x i minus x bar, times y i.

40
00:07:51,054 --> 00:08:02,979
Each y value gets its x deviation as a weight. Every value in group 0 gets minus n 2 over n. Every value in group 1 gets n 1 over n.

41
00:08:03,429 --> 00:08:21,755
The sum of the y values in group 0 is n 1 times y bar 0. In the example, 18 equals 4 times 4.5. The sum in group 1 is n 2 times y bar 1. In the example, 24 equals 3 times 8.

42
00:08:22,205 --> 00:08:45,245
Take out n 1 n 2 over n. S x y equals n 1 n 2 over n, times y bar 1 minus y bar 0. In the example: minus 3 over 7 times 18, plus 4 over 7 times 24, is 6. The formula gives 12 over 7 times 3.5, also 6.

43
00:08:45,695 --> 00:08:55,231
Step 4 of card A. Divide S x y by S x x. The factor n 1 n 2 over n is on the top and on the bottom.

44
00:08:55,681 --> 00:09:08,950
It cancels. So beta 1 hat is y bar 1 minus y bar 0. In the example, 8 minus 4.5, which is 3.5. The green arrow shows this difference.

45
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:29,048
For beta 0 hat we need y bar, the mean of all n values. It is n 1 y bar 0 plus n 2 y bar 1, over n. In the example, 18 plus 24 is 42, and 42 over 7 is 6. The ring marks the point x bar, y bar.

46
00:09:29,498 --> 00:09:47,205
Now beta 0 hat is y bar, minus beta 1 hat times x bar. Put in beta 1 hat from line 2 and x bar equal to n 2 over n. In the example, 6 minus 3.5 times 3 over 7 is 4.5.

47
00:09:47,655 --> 00:09:53,372
Put everything over n. The two terms n 2 y bar 1 have opposite signs.

48
00:09:53,822 --> 00:10:07,411
They cancel. What is left is n 1 plus n 2, times y bar 0, over n. n 1 plus n 2 is n. So beta 0 hat is y bar 0, the mean of group 0.

49
00:10:07,861 --> 00:10:19,467
This is the result. beta 0 hat is the mean of group 0. beta 1 hat is the difference of the two group means. The fitted line goes through both group means.

50
00:10:19,917 --> 00:10:43,703
Here is a second way to do step 4. Write the least squares criterion S. For group 0, x i is 0, so each term is y i minus beta 0, squared. For group 1, x i is 1, so each term is y i minus beta 0 minus beta 1, squared. The gray line is one candidate line, with its distances.

51
00:10:44,153 --> 00:10:58,319
Call beta 0 plus beta 1 by the name m. Then the first sum uses only beta 0, and the second sum uses only m. The labels in the picture show which parameter each group uses.

52
00:10:58,769 --> 00:11:09,094
So we can make each sum a minimum on its own. Take the derivative with respect to beta 0. Only the first sum, group 0, has beta 0.

53
00:11:09,544 --> 00:11:23,453
Set it to 0. The sum of the y i in group 0 equals n 1 beta 0 hat. Divide by n 1. beta 0 hat is y bar 0, which is 4.5 in the example.

54
00:11:23,903 --> 00:11:30,645
The same steps for the second sum give m hat equal to y bar 1, which is 8 in the example.

55
00:11:31,095 --> 00:11:42,999
Then beta 1 hat is m hat minus beta 0 hat: y bar 1 minus y bar 0. The orange residuals are now the distances to the group means.

56
00:11:43,449 --> 00:11:47,225
This is the same answer. Use either way on the exam.

57
00:11:47,675 --> 00:12:01,179
Step 5 of card A: the t test, with the example. The fitted values are the group means. So each residual is y i minus its group mean. The picture shows the seven residuals.

58
00:12:01,629 --> 00:12:07,303
Square them and add. Group 0 gives 5. Group 1 gives 2. The sum is 7.

59
00:12:07,753 --> 00:12:12,980
Divide by n minus 2, which is 5. sigma hat squared is 1.4.

60
00:12:13,430 --> 00:12:31,734
The standard error of beta 1 hat is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. The square root of 1.4, over the square root of 12 over 7, is 0.9037. The curve is the t density with 5 degrees of freedom.

61
00:12:32,184 --> 00:12:45,816
The t statistic is 3.5 minus 0, over 0.9037. This is 3.873. The blue areas show values at least this far from 0.

62
00:12:46,266 --> 00:13:04,762
The critical value t 0.975 with 5 degrees of freedom is 2.571. On the exam, this quantile is given. 3.873 is larger. The red tails show the rejection region at the 5 percent level.

63
00:13:05,212 --> 00:13:17,948
The two-sided p-value is 2 times the probability that T 5 is at least 3.873. It is 0.0117, less than 0.05.

64
00:13:18,398 --> 00:13:39,390
So we reject the null hypothesis at the 5 percent level. The data give evidence that the two group means are different. The orange box is optional, and not on the slides. 1 over S x x is 1 over n 1 plus 1 over n 2. So this regression test is the pooled two-sample t test.

65
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:54,944
Section 3. Question type B changes the x variable to a new scale. Then it asks for the meaning of the new coefficients and for the p-values. Assignment 1, question 2, parts b and c, is of this type.

66
00:13:55,394 --> 00:14:08,301
Method card B has five steps. Step 1: solve the new variable for x. For the standardized x star, x i equals x bar plus s x times x i star.

67
00:14:08,751 --> 00:14:21,871
Step 2: put this into the mean, beta 0 plus beta 1 x i, and group the terms. This gives the new intercept and the new slope. The red and purple points are the two intercepts.

68
00:14:22,321 --> 00:14:32,667
Step 3: interpret the new slope. It is the change in mean y for one unit of x star. One unit of x star is s x units of x.

69
00:14:33,117 --> 00:14:47,048
Step 4: the fitted values, the residuals, and sigma hat do not change. S x star x star is S x x over s x squared, so the standard error is multiplied by s x.

70
00:14:47,498 --> 00:14:57,269
Step 5: the factor s x cancels in t. The degrees of freedom are n minus 2 in both models. So the p-value is the same.

71
00:14:57,719 --> 00:15:19,009
Assignment 1, question 2, parts b and c. Model A is the usual model in x. Model C uses a new variable, x star. x star is x minus x bar, over s x. Part b asks for the meaning of beta 1 C. Part c compares the two p-values. Pause the video and try it.

72
00:15:19,459 --> 00:15:34,371
Step 1 of card B: see x star as a new scale for x. The pictures use the brainhead data. x is head size in cubic centimeters. The x star ruler measures the same head sizes in a new unit.

73
00:15:34,821 --> 00:15:45,424
At x equal to x bar, 3637.9, x star is 0. So x star is 0 at the centre of the data.

74
00:15:45,874 --> 00:16:03,197
One step on the x star ruler is s x, which is 361.1 cubic centimeters. s x is the square root of S x x over n minus 1. So one unit of x star is one standard deviation of x.

75
00:16:03,647 --> 00:16:22,569
Step 1 of card B. Solve the definition of x star for x. Multiply by s x and add x bar. x i equals x bar, plus s x times x i star. The picture shows the mean line of the brainhead data, with the x axis from 0.

76
00:16:23,019 --> 00:16:37,761
Step 2. Put this x i into the mean of Model A. In Model A, the intercept beta 0 is the mean of y at x equal to 0. For head size, x equal to 0 is far outside the data.

77
00:16:38,211 --> 00:16:50,221
Multiply out and group the terms. The constant term is beta 0 plus beta 1 x bar. This is the mean of y at x equal to x bar, the purple point.

78
00:16:50,671 --> 00:17:07,333
Match the terms with Model C. The constant is the intercept of Model C. The coefficient of x i star, s x times beta 1, is the slope of Model C. The triangle shows it: the rise over a run of s x.

79
00:17:07,783 --> 00:17:12,945
So both models describe the same mean line. Only the x scale changes.

80
00:17:13,395 --> 00:17:21,950
Part b, step 3 of card B. Here is the brainhead scatter plot. The x axis is head size in cubic centimeters.

81
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:31,104
Under it is the x star axis. Its 0 is at x bar. Each step of 1 is one standard deviation of head size.

82
00:17:31,554 --> 00:17:34,775
The fitted line is the same line in both scales.

83
00:17:35,225 --> 00:18:02,681
beta 1 C is the difference in mean brain weight between two populations whose head sizes differ by one sample standard deviation, s x. For these data, beta 1 C hat is s x times beta 1 hat: 361.13 times 0.26082, which is 94.19 grams. beta 0 C is the mean of y at x equal to x bar.

84
00:18:03,131 --> 00:18:16,123
Step 4 of card B: fit Model C with the least squares formulas. Line 1: the x star values have mean 0. The bottom panel shows Model C, with 0 at the centre.

85
00:18:16,573 --> 00:18:28,691
Line 2: S x star x star is S x x over s x squared. This is n minus 1, because s x squared is S x x over n minus 1.

86
00:18:29,141 --> 00:18:38,698
Line 3: each x star deviation is the x deviation over s x. So S x star y is S x y over s x.

87
00:18:39,148 --> 00:18:51,927
Line 4: divide. beta 1 C hat is s x times beta 1 hat, 94.19 for the brainhead data. The estimates follow the same rule as the parameters.

88
00:18:52,377 --> 00:19:07,694
Line 5: beta 0 C hat is y bar, minus beta 1 C hat times the mean of x star. That mean is 0. So beta 0 C hat is y bar, 1284.3.

89
00:19:08,144 --> 00:19:21,051
Line 6: the fitted values. y bar plus beta 1 hat times x i minus x bar is the Model A fitted value. The orange residuals are equal in both panels.

90
00:19:21,501 --> 00:19:34,045
So the two models have the same fitted values and the same residuals. So sigma hat is also the same: 72.35 on 234 degrees of freedom.

91
00:19:34,495 --> 00:19:46,911
Part c, step 5 of card B. The test of beta 1 equals 0 in Model A uses t A: beta 1 hat, over sigma hat over the square root of S x x.

92
00:19:47,361 --> 00:20:01,483
Step 4 first: the standard error in Model C. sigma hat is the same, and S x star x star is n minus 1. So the standard error is sigma hat over the square root of n minus 1.

93
00:20:01,933 --> 00:20:12,536
n minus 1 is S x x over s x squared. Put it in. The standard error is s x sigma hat, over the square root of S x x.

94
00:20:12,986 --> 00:20:24,442
sigma hat over the square root of S x x is the standard error of Model A. So the Model C standard error is s x times the Model A standard error.

95
00:20:24,892 --> 00:20:36,540
Step 5. t C is beta 1 C hat over its standard error. The top is s x times beta 1 hat. The bottom is s x times the standard error.

96
00:20:36,990 --> 00:20:40,745
The factor s x cancels. t C equals t A.

97
00:20:41,195 --> 00:21:01,248
Both statistics have a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom under the null hypothesis. The same value and the same distribution give the same p-value. Also, beta 1 C is 0 exactly when beta 1 is 0, so the two null hypotheses say the same thing.

98
00:21:01,698 --> 00:21:19,575
Check this with the brainhead data. Here is the Model A table from Lecture 3, page 45. The residual standard error is 72.35 on 234 degrees of freedom. Step 4 of the card says it is the same in Model C.

99
00:21:20,025 --> 00:21:47,524
We fit Model C to the same data. The slope estimate is 94.19, which is 361.13 times 0.26082. The standard error is 4.720, which is 361.13 times 0.01307. The t value is 19.957 in both tables. So the p-values are equal.

100
00:21:47,974 --> 00:22:08,710
The intercept rows are different. Model A tests whether the mean at x equal 0 is 0, the red point. Model C tests whether the mean at x bar is 0, the purple point. These are different hypotheses, at different points on the line. So each intercept test answers its own question.

101
00:22:09,160 --> 00:22:23,752
The confidence intervals agree too. The 95 percent interval for beta 1 C is s x times the interval for beta 1. Both intervals exclude 0. This agrees with the equal p-values.

102
00:22:24,202 --> 00:22:35,125
The same card works for any linear change of x. This slide is a summary, and not on the slides. The blue ruler is head size in cubic centimeters.

103
00:22:35,575 --> 00:22:55,841
Units of 100 cubic centimeters: x prime is x over 100. One unit of x prime is 100 units of x. So the slope is multiplied by 100: 26.08 grams per 100 cubic centimeters. The t value does not change.

104
00:22:56,291 --> 00:23:08,238
The standardized x star from the assignment is x minus x bar, over s x. The slope is multiplied by s x. The t value again does not change.

105
00:23:08,688 --> 00:23:34,373
In general, for x prime equal to x minus a, over b, the slope and its standard error are multiplied by b. The t statistic keeps its size, so the two-sided p-value is the same. If b is negative, t changes sign. For example, swap the group codes 0 and 1 in Tutorial 2: the slope changes sign. The new intercept is the mean of y at x equal to a.

106
00:23:34,823 --> 00:23:46,599
Section 4. Question type C gives a test result and asks about the confidence interval, or the reverse. Assignment 1, question 2, part a, is of this type.

107
00:23:47,049 --> 00:24:06,079
Method card C has four steps. Step 1: at level 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis beta 1 equals 0 when the absolute value of t is larger than t 0.975 with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

108
00:24:06,529 --> 00:24:16,171
Step 2: multiply both sides by the standard error. The left side becomes the absolute value of beta 1 hat minus 0.

109
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:29,784
Step 3: read the two sides on the number line. The left side is the distance from 0 to beta 1 hat. The right side is the half-width of the 95 percent confidence interval.

110
00:24:30,234 --> 00:24:39,429
Step 4: so we reject at level 0.05 exactly when 0 is outside the 95 percent confidence interval.

111
00:24:39,879 --> 00:24:57,756
Assignment 1, question 2, part a. A two-sided test of beta 1 equals 0 rejects at the 0.05 level. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: the rejection means the absolute t is larger than the quantile.

112
00:24:58,206 --> 00:25:05,950
Step 2: multiply both sides by the standard error. It is positive, so the inequality keeps its direction.

113
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:16,661
Step 3: the left side is the distance from 0 to the centre of the interval. The right side is the half-width. So 0 is outside the interval.

114
00:25:17,111 --> 00:25:24,642
Step 4, the answer: the 95 percent confidence interval for beta 1 does not contain 0.

115
00:25:25,092 --> 00:25:47,855
Check with the two-group example. The half-width is 2.571 times 0.9037, which is 2.323. The interval is 1.177 to 5.823. It does not contain 0, and t, 3.873, is larger than 2.571.

116
00:25:48,305 --> 00:26:04,945
Check with the brainhead data. t is 19.957, larger than 1.970. The interval is 0.2351 to 0.2866. It does not contain 0 either.

117
00:26:05,395 --> 00:26:29,139
To recap. Card A, two groups coded 0 and 1. beta 0 is the mean of group 0, and beta 1 is the difference of the group means. The least squares estimates are y bar 0 and y bar 1 minus y bar 0. A test of no difference is the t test of beta 1 equals 0, with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

118
00:26:29,589 --> 00:26:51,754
Card B, a new scale for x. With a standardized x, beta 1 C is s x times beta 1: the mean difference per one standard deviation of x. The fitted values and sigma hat do not change. The slope and its standard error are both multiplied by s x, so t and the p-value do not change.

119
00:26:52,204 --> 00:27:08,268
Card C, a test and a confidence interval. A two-sided test rejects at level 0.05 exactly when 0 is outside the 95 percent interval. This is the end of the Midterm 1 review.
